[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Automated Market Makers (AMMs): From Constant Product to Concentrated Liquidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Automated Market Makers (AMMs) have revolutionized decentralized finance (DeFi) by replacing traditional order books with mathematical formulas. This tutorial explores the architectural evolution from Uniswap v2's constant product model to Uniswap v3 and v4's concentrated liquidity paradigms."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Uniswap v2 Constant Product Model"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The foundational model of modern AMMs is the Constant Product Market Maker, defined by the invariant equation x * y = k, where x and y represent the reserves of two tokens in a liquidity pool, and k remains constant during trades (excluding swap fees)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Solidity representation of the constant product invariant
uint256 constant balanceProduct = reserveX * reserveY;
require(newReserveX * newReserveY >= balanceProduct, "K invariant violated");"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Liquidity Providers (LPs) deposit an equivalent value of both tokens across the entire price spectrum from zero to infinity."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Traders experience slippage that scales non-linearly with the size of the trade relative to total pool reserves."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Capital inefficiency is a major drawback, as the vast majority of liquidity (prices near zero or infinity) is never utilized."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Uniswap v3: Concentrated Liquidity"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Uniswap v3 introduced concentrated liquidity, allowing LPs to allocate capital within custom price ranges [P_min, P_max]. This concentrates capital where market activity actually happens, dramatically increasing capital efficiency."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Virtual reserves concept in Uniswap v3
// LPs provide liquidity within a specific tick range [sqrtPriceLower, sqrtPriceUpper]
sqrtPriceX96 currentPrice = SqrtPriceMath.getNextSqrtPriceFromInput(
    sqrtPriceX96,
    liquidity,
    amountIn,
    zeroForOne
);"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ticks represent discrete price points along the price continuum."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Positions are represented as non-fungible tokens (NFTs) rather than ERC-20 LP tokens due to unique price ranges."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Active liquidity management becomes necessary to avoid out-of-range positions and impermanent loss amplification."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Uniswap v4: Hooks and Singleton Architecture"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Uniswap v4 revolutionizes the architecture further by introducing the Singleton pattern (where all pools reside in a single smart contract to save gas on multi-hop trades) and Hooks, which allow developers to execute custom logic at defined points in the pool lifecycle."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a basic Uniswap v4 Hook implementation
contract CustomFeeHook is BaseHook {
    function beforeSwap(address sender, PoolKey calldata key, IPoolManager.SwapParams calldata params, bytes calldata data)
        external override returns (bytes4) {
        // Implement custom dynamic fee logic here
        return BaseHook.beforeSwap.selector;
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Singleton Contract: Reduces gas costs for pool creation and token routing by eliminating multi-contract transfers."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hooks: Enable dynamic fees, on-chain limit orders, custom oracle integrations, and TWAMM (Time-Weighted Average Market Maker) functionalities."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Conclusion: AMM design has evolved from rigid, capital-inefficient formulas into modular, highly customizable execution environments. Understanding constant products, concentrated liquidity ranges, and hook-based extensions is essential for modern Web3 developers."}]
